        },
        {
          "widget": {
            "name": "about-method",
            "multilineTextboxSpec": {
              "lines": [
                "**What a tier means.** Each planning area's tier crosses the forecast heat level for the day (NEA's heat-stress categories: Low, Moderate, High) with how vulnerable the area is, its third among the 32 rated areas:\n",
                "\n",
                "| Forecast heat (NEA) / vulnerability | Lower third | Middle third | Upper third |\n",
                "|---|---|---|---|\n",
                "| Low | Low | Low | Low |\n",
                "| Moderate | Low | Medium | Medium |\n",
                "| High | Medium | High | High |\n",
                "\n",
                "Heat is forecast per planning area, from its nearest WBGT station; blocks rank vulnerability only, never heat. The forecast's probability only orders areas inside a tier and is never shown.\n",
                "\n",
                "**How vulnerability is measured.** Seven measures, each turned into a percentile among the rated areas (higher means more vulnerable) and weighted as in the table below: residents aged 75+ and their share of residents; 1-2 room rental flats; a small hot-month rise in 1-2 room electricity use (may indicate less air-con use); HDB flats beyond 400 m of a refuge; refuges per 1,000 residents aged 65+; HDB flats beyond 400 m of a park. A share or a rate over fewer than 1,000 people or flats is held at the middle (0.5); counts keep their value. The top 11 areas are the upper third, the next 11 the middle, the last 10 the lower. Areas without HDB blocks are not rated and show the heat level only.\n"
              ]
            }
          },
          "position": {
            "x": 0,
            "y": 1,
            "width": 12,
            "height": 10
          }
        },
